    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d71">

                <pb facs="0137" n="47" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg47"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1110</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Vladivostok</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vladivostok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-02-17T19:00:00+08:47"><hi rendition="#italic">February 17,
                                1918, 7 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 17, 5.45
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Peking requests I transmit copy of the following telegram sent Legation by Major
                    Drysdale: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>February 15, 2 p.m. Probable German agents inciting Bolsheviks and labor
                            organizations and for purchase supplies for Germans. Prisoners of war
                            being released. Their organization possible menace <pb facs="0138"
                                n="48" xml:id="pg48"/> Allied interests. Advise financial assistance
                            to organization of Zabaikal Cossacks under Semenov and Ussuri Cossacks
                            under Mendrin [Kalmykov?] as railway guard with Ustrugov<anchor n="1"
                                xml:id="d71-f1"/> as head. Necessary expenditures to be supervised.
                            These forces if assisted financially will have little difficulty in
                            controlling zone of railway from Vladivostok to Karymskaya thus insuring
                            protection Allied interests. American, British, and French Consuls
                            Vladivostok and Harbin all advise some such effort necessary. I can see
                            no probability of explanation [compromising] our interests by such a
                            move and our interests certainly would be protected thereby. If scheme
                            of financial assistance approved my return Harbin necessary immediately.
                            Please advise me United States Consulate at Vladivostok.
                        Drysdale.</p></quote></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d71-f1" n="1" xml:id="d71-f1-content">
                <p>L. A. Ustrugov, formerly Assistant Minister of Ways of Communication under
                    Kerensky; Feb. 9, 1918, Minister of Ways of Communication in the provisional
                    Siberian government.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
